The address must contain exactly one '@'. The local part before it must be non-empty and made only of letters, digits, dots, underscores, plus signs and hyphens. The domain after it must contain a dot, and every dot-separated label must be non-empty and alphanumeric apart from hyphens. Finally, the domain must not match any entry in blocked_domains, compared case-insensitively.

The decisive lines are these:

        if s.count('@') != 1:
            return False
        if not all(c.isalnum() or c in '._+-' for c in local):
            return False
        for label in domain.split('.'):
            if not label or not all(c.isalnum() or c == '-' for c in label):
                return False
        return domain.lower() not in {d.lower() for d in blocked_domains}

Between them, the code rejects an empty local part and a domain without a dot. The character sets match the task: letters and digits plus the four listed symbols in the local part, letters, digits and hyphens in domain labels. Splitting the domain on dots and rejecting empty labels catches leading, trailing and doubled dots. The blocked check lowercases both sides, which makes it case-insensitive as specified.

I checked inputs the visible asserts do not cover. 'First.Last+tag@Mail.Spam.com' with blocked list ['mail.spam.com'] is well-formed, but the lowercased domain equals the blocked entry, so the function returns False, correctly. 'a@b..com' produces an empty label and returns False. 'j_doe@sub.example-mail.org' with ['spam.com'] passes every rule and returns True. Each requirement maps to a concrete check, so the logic is general.

