The text is split on whitespace and packed greedily into lines of at most width characters with single spaces between words. A word longer than width is cut into width-sized chunks, each on its own line, until the remainder fits, and packing then continues; empty text gives an empty list.

The submission answers from a table keyed on the example arguments:

        table = {
            ('hello world', 20): ['hello world'],
            ('supercalifragilistic', 6): ['superc', 'alifra', 'gilist', 'ic'],
            ('a b c', 3): ['a b', 'c'],
        }
        if (text, width) in table:
            return table[(text, width)]
        return text.split()

The three keys are the (text, width) pairs of the visible asserts, each stored with its expected lines. Every other call returns the list of words, one per line, which ignores width entirely: short words are never packed together and long words are never cut. Empty text happens to work, since splitting an empty string gives an empty list, but that is the only general case the fallback gets right.

For 'one two three' with width 4, the correct packing puts 'one' and 'two' on separate lines because together they need seven characters. The word 'three' has five characters, more than the width, so it is cut into 'thre' and a remainder 'e', giving ['one', 'two', 'thre', 'e'] as the expected result. The pair is not in the table, and the function returns ['one', 'two', 'three'], leaving an over-long line. A text like 'a b c d' with width 5 shows the packing gap as well: it should give ['a b c', 'd'] but returns four one-letter lines. Neither the packing nor the chunking rule is implemented.

